{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/550680-arcane-s-meditation-on-heroes-from-primordial","id":550680,"title":"Arcane's Meditation on Heroes from Primordial","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Beast: The Primordial"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-06-11T14:11:10-07:00","id":550680,"title":"Arcane's Meditation on Heroes from Primordial","content":"I should be writing on the Hedge, but screw it, I want to meditate on heroes away from other contexts. Arcane Arts don't care, Arcane Arts do what he wants.<br /> <br /> So, with that said, moving away from the context of <i>Beast the Primordial</i>, what do you think of when you think of the word heroes?<br /> <br /> Well, if you're anything like me*, you probably think of superheroes like Captain America, Spiderman, Thor, Superman, etc., etc. I mean, there are other people you probably think of, but culture is kind of glutted on the subject right now, thanks to the American film industry cashing in on our youth and going somewhere with it. Lots of big epic fight scenes, tough moral choices, triumph and all that stuff.<br /> <br /> But for me, stuck in the corners of the subject, for me, I see some peculiar scenes. A lot of them are from Superman, who isn't my favorite superhero, and they're from the comics, which I pretty much explicitly don't read, and yet these kind of form a true 'heart' of the genre for me. One is where it's just Supes talking a kid down from a roof, convincing them to rethink things rather than committing suicide. Another is from a security guard saying &quot;Oh, wow, guess the park doesn't get safer with you two around&quot; to him and Nightwing, and Supes responds &quot;You mean the three of us.&quot; It's one part gentle rebuke and one part supremely awesome inclusion, that Superman considers his contributions meaningful. A final one from Supes is just him getting a cat out of a tree for an old lady. Like, that's it. It's cliche, and yet he does it.<br /> <br /> Another moment for me that stands out is Andrew Garfield's Spiderman, who walks a kid home, just chatting to him. That was the first time, in a Spiderman movie, where I said to myself &quot;I would want to be this character&quot;. Oh, and another one is just Captain America, comics wise again, just talking about the valor of the French resistance in World War 2 and explaining that he'll always love the people for that. That was a good moment that almost makes me want to read more comics. These sorts of moments and more from other works, like Madoka Kaname from Madoka Magica or Hikaru Shidou at the end of the Rayearth manga, hit this sort of notion that says &quot;This-this is what is admirable about heroism.&quot;<br /> <br /> I imagine a lot of us have these sorts, and not just in superheroes. I think we all carry these sorts of things.<br /> <br /> ...But they come second to a whole lot of other things. You think heroes, and massive, crime fighting epics come in now. And something unsettling occurs. You question the heroes, and the heroism itself.<br /> <br /> There was an article somewhere, I think from the blogs on <a href=\"http://www.starpowercomic.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Star Power </a>**, where one of the writers asks why it is that so many super heroes have something tragic kick off their heroic career, and that settled on me when I read it. It's not just superheroes, it's fiction in general. Spiderman would have been a poor sideshow wrestling icon at best and possibly a self-entitled douchebag villain if Uncle Ben hadn't died, his story seems to suggest. Luke Skywalker has a desire to fight and make a difference, but he rejects it when given the chance to care, and only goes once he's left with nowhere to go. Batman is born from the cooling corpse of Bruce Wayne's childhood, laid low by two falling bodies that used to be his parents applied directly to his memory. Superman apparently has to kill Zod in order to know that he'll never kill in the last movie. Captain America is an oddity worth evaluating, because he didn't have something tragic happen to make him Captain America-he just didn't like bullies. How many stories do we know of that start with the hero losing something, with something scarring them for life?<br /> <br /> Why can't a good guy...just be a good guy? Why can't a hero just be born from having the opportunity to make a difference, and taking that shot? They exist, but nowhere near as much as the tragedy-born heroes. Do I have to steal to know it's wrong and work to stop theives?*** Does someone have to shove their hand down the front of my pants and both of us suffer mental scars from violation before we can be a dynamic duo? It doesn't even have to be personal. Do I have to see a car crash to know that speeding will fuck a person up? Do I have to lose my mother before I have the conviction to do what is right? <i>WHY IS THIS A THING?</i><br /> <br /> Part of this is that it creates flaws and holes and trigger points and such-it makes characters more human and makes the heroic struggle more dramatic, because there are torture points to be pushed and used for tension and effect. But are these things needed in and of themselves? Does Hercules story have to begin with him slaughtering his kids and doing impossible tasks out of penance? Does Gilgamesh really have to lose Enkidu and immortality before he stops being a colossal dick?<br /> <br /> The other thing that's sort of related is how divorced what I talked about at the beginning is the minimal. Our heroes are almost always violent, or some simulacrum of it. Even My Little Pony, a show for little girls(ostensibly) and spends most of it's time on slice of life comedic problems, invested heavily opening and closing episodes with the conflict being resolved by giant rainbow lasers of reprogramming. It took FIVE SEASONS for a major arc to happen that didn't resolve itself with a more violent answer. Hell, even the Care Bear Movies that had the original &quot;TASTE THE RAINBOW MOTHERFUCKER&quot; moments never resolved their huge ass problems with the Care Bear Stare-they legitimately solved it by, well, caring.<br /> <br /> Human vs Human is, for a lot of people, more dynamic and interesting that Human vs World, but how much of our idea of Hero versus Human wrapped up in &quot;BEAT THE SHIT OUT OF THE GUY?&quot; Is this part of the reason why we have tragic openings? SO that the audience and the heroes feel justified in beating the shit out of someone? Is that really the ideal we're selling? &quot;You hurt me, and I'm moved by a sense of righteousness, so it's okay for me to beat you into a pulp&quot;?<br /> <br /> I'm a fan of the recent Fate/Zero and Fate/Stay Night animes because it asks questions about these ideas. Noble Saber, King Arturia, pulled the sword out of the stone with the ambition of leading her nation into unity and happiness, and she's openly called a Heroic Spirit. But so is Gille De Rais, the child-murdering occultist from the Thirty Years War, and Gilgamesh, who seems to have lost the humbling from his original story and instead plays off like he does at the beginning. THe Hashashin, Medusa, Circe make for a dark cast, and Alexander the Great, Cuchulainn, Lancelot, and more of a greyer cast-a broad range of people are called Heroic Spirits right alongside the righteous King of Knights. Kiristugu, one of hte protagonists from Fate/Zero, has the same sort of tragic opening that makes a hero in most of fiction, and aspires to his heroic ideals....but never calls himself a hero. Shirou Emiya must confront Archer as the idealization and full commitment of his heroic ideals. With Shirou, they are, if misplaced, still something worthy of admiration and mimicry, but Archer is an ugly character in terms of his outlook.<br /> <br /> All of this leads to a question. Is the idea of heroism that we are sold so often, everyday, throughout the whole of human history, really the sort of Heroes we want to be? Or do we want to be a different kind of hero, one that aspires to make things better, ones that might use violence, but aren't <i>defined by violence</i>, and furthermore, violence solely justified by tragedy? Don't we want heroes who are heroes because that's the sort of people we want to be?<br /> <br /> It brings to mind a image I came across a while ago, where at the top, the headline and image from a newspaper clip was shown. A rape victim had went and found her rapist, and set him on fire. What followed was a lot of great comments from the internet, parodying the common shit thrown at raped women, like &quot;Well, if he didn't want to catch fire he shouldn't have worn such flammable clothes&quot;. It was hilarious, and at the time I approved of it. But I think back now, and I wonder if the rapist died, and if he didn't, what was he doing with himself now, and what he might have done if he hadn't been set on fire. And I had to ask myself &quot;While he deserves punishment, did he deserve that?&quot; And I had to ask myself &quot;How right is she to do that?&quot;<br /> <br /> What <i>is</i> heroism? Is our ideal in fiction who we really want to be?<br /> <br /> I've never had much problem with Heroes in Beast: The Primordial. I understand why people do, and I have faltered at justifying it or explaining it several times. The responses are critical and keen. But I think there's just as much value is a piece of work criticizing this notion of heroism we have, the one where a person gets hurt, and so hurts the right people in kind, and is praised for it. I think we have a right to acknowledge that &quot;Heroes&quot; are as much the problem as &quot;True Fae&quot;****.<br /> <br /> Just because you got hurt, doesn't mean it's right to hurt others. Does it?<br /> <br /> *, I am so sorry.<br /> <br /> **Which, by the way, is an awesome Sci-Fi superhero comic.<br /> <br /> ***f so, something went wrong in my early teen years, but let's not go there.<br /> <br /> ****And that &quot;changelings&quot; can contribute as much as &quot;Beasts&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T14:27:34-07:00","id":550712,"title":"","content":"Agreed, although i will say i find it interesting that the modern interpretation of &quot;Heroes&quot; is always too have them be Shining examples. David and Goliath isn't a Story of a Hero vs a Villain it's a Story of two Heroes fighting, Goliath was a Hero to his people and was killed for it. That's the interpretation I got of Heroes from my Literature studies.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Light and Dark are two Sides of a Coin... Humanity is What Happens when it Lands on it's Edge.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3981-rickmanuk","name":"RickmanUK","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3981_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"533"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T14:33:23-07:00","id":550718,"title":"","content":"The World of Darkness usually takes a skeptical view of the idea that the solution to a problem is violence, even when other solutions don't readily suggest themselves. Violent action is often the very thing that makes our headlining monsters <i>monsters</i>, and it's rare that the games are going to laud someone as a hero (small 'h') specifically because they stood up to beat the tar out of someone.<br /> <br /> <i>Hunter</i>, particularly, lives at the intersection of &quot;killing people is questionable&quot; and &quot;I don't have any other way to help.&quot; Heroes (capital 'H') are a different side of that problem.<br /> <br /> None of the games presents a pat answer to these questions or an easy solution to these problems, though some are more fraught than others.<br /> <br /> (EDIT: Note that &quot;I hurt people because I'm hungry,&quot; an issue in <i>Vampire </i>and <i>Beast</i>, is another angle on the same problems. No pat answers.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Cavaliers of Mars Creator</b><br /> <b>Retired CofD Lead</b><br /> <br /> Check out my <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Rose%20Bailey\" target=\"_blank\">guides to </a><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/261142/BITE-ME-How-to-Write-Vampire?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> and my <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/4895/Fantasy-Heartbreaker?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">indie games</a>!","edited":{"userid":2,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","user":"Rose Bailey","date":". 06-11-2015, 05:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":11}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","name":"Rose Bailey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2361"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T14:46:57-07:00","id":550755,"title":"","content":"I think a lot of the confusion arise from the fact that we use the word &quot;Hero&quot; to refer to people who are the ideal embodiment of at least some of a society's values. Thing is, values are differents depending on the time, place, and circumstances and thus, so are definitions of heroism.<br /> On the other hand, heroes often act as if they were above those values. They break laws, violate boundaries, betray allies.<br /> I believe the heroic condition really depends on this paradoxal state as flawed paragons of subjectives virtues.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>When will you rage?","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1405-anteros","name":"Anteros","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1405_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"661"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T15:12:51-07:00","id":550785,"title":"","content":"When I think of the defining traits of a Hero, two key aspects leap to mind. Those being a larger than life motivation, and an unyielding drive to pursue it. Confidence is also common, as is self-righteousness, but not strictly essential.<br /> <br /> Now what qualifies as larger than life varies from story to story. In The Illiad that Odysseus's motivation is largely to return home, not grand at all until you add the fact that he has to overcome Poseidon to do it. Conversely Beowulf appears to have little motivation beyond drinking and fighting, until you realize that his motivation is effectively, &quot;never back down from a challenge and never be shown up&quot; in which case it seems implausibly grand. A more recent example would be Wilson Fisk, the villain in Netflix's Daredevil. Though an antagonist Fisk has a driving, overwhelming motivation and a towering objective. In the end he fails, but not for lack of a heroic effort.<br /> <br /> Deep down Heroes <b>know what they want </b>even if they cannot voice it, they are not the sort to be swayed or dissuaded. A Hero is her own person, not necessarily a nice person, or a good person, but profoundly their <b>own</b> person. And though they may be occasionally lead astray (generally somewhere in act 2) they always find their way back (usually right before the climax).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Onyx Path Moderator </span><br /> <b>Mod Voice is <u><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">RED</span></u>*</b><br /> <i>*All other colors should be read aloud in the voice of Don Knotts</i><b>.</b> <hr /><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/18519-pendragon-s-homebrew-hub\" target=\"_blank\">Hacks and House Rules Hub Thread</a></b><br /> <br /> <b>CofD Hacks By Splat: <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/13vBLaS5Ua1Ez-cVyK6uFa4ssvYeIIZpoA5GQTXytfcA/edit\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\">Vampire</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sqltG7X-C_MdEe00PtVOZmki8V_K1M1FpQtLwTvb2jg/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#006400\">Werewolf</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1bpr1EjQ4RfZ5dz-tXmJJ93P2f_l7fUIUwpPLWs9Z-SI/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800080\">Mage</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vKpSTktCUuUaqxXncG4txQPtvPclzXlFZEEX7tbrTSg/edit#\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Mummy</span></a> | <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Spynbcs_VgCDHdooKWXobZNe6C9CMjONeRTlvXIq9fI/edit?usp=drive_web\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Demon</span></a></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/148-pendragon","name":"PenDragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar148_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1657"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T17:10:20-07:00","id":550879,"title":"","content":"I just want to put out three things on this.<br /> <br /> 1) I woke up with this sort of rambling thought going n that was demanding to weave together into something, so this was more or less done to get this out of my head so I can focus on other things.<br /> <br /> 2) My thesis*, if I have one, is that the traditional structure of heroes throughout our fiction is problematic enough to warrant scrutiny in this sort of dialogue. I'm not justifying Beasts here(of the two, I think they are the more problematic of the central cycle of this game), just saying that this strange reliance on &quot;Good Guys come from Tragic Backstories&quot; and it's uncomfortable juxtaposition towards 'Heroic Violence' is an idea worth...if not attacking, then at least examining very closely. I would posit that there's room in a later supplement for &quot;True Heroes&quot; who make Beasts really sweat, partially because they're a little more unpredictable in their potential judgment and action, but I don't make those sorts of calls and I'm not quite sure it has room as a major thing in the World of Darkness.<br /> <br /> 3) Also, and this just occurred to me, I feel like the archetypalization of this really weird mix of tropes and their prominence in fiction lends towards some resolution on the &quot;Heroes are bland&quot; front for anyone who might read this. A skeleton is a thing you frame a person on, and that's what the books delivers. Maybe there needs to be more than bones from the writers(Sleeping Beauty, though!), maybe fans need to remember the plethora of options. Whatever.<br /> <br /> I probably haven't said anything new, but it wasn't leaving me alone, so, yeah.<br /> <br /> *I say this because the original post is really damn rambly, not because I think it's incomprehensible. Also making that clear.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 06-11-2015, 08:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T19:21:57-07:00","id":550988,"title":"","content":"Also if the concept of &quot;Heroic Violence&quot; is problematic in modern stories it is even more problematic in the classics.<br /> <br /> Gilgamesh, Hercules, Oedipus, Siegfried commit great crimes during their lives, often justified in their minds by their heroic deeds (or more rarely prompting the heroic deeds in penance). They were as cautionary tales as figures to be emulated<br /> <br /> Seriously even Greek and Latin authors questioned the acts of their heroes so the characterization of Beast is nothing really shocking on that point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2225-ganonso","name":"ganonso","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2225_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-11T20:59:48-07:00","id":551046,"title":"","content":"While not too pertinent to the topic of this meditation I'm greatly reminded of one particular Extra Credits video I saw in regard to the classical hero myths. I'll post the link below and I feel it's definitely worth a watch since it does examine the strengths an weaknesses of using such an Archetype in games, especially when it comes to player agency. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=3IyPc5nw0Wk\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=3IyPc5nw0Wk\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=3IyPc5nw0Wk\" target=\"_blank\"> https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=3IyPc5nw0Wk </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T08:39:08-07:00","id":551576,"title":"","content":"I want to point out that there is a difference between a hero and a super hero. Heroes are humans that go above and beyond the call of duty for the good of people. With a super hero, the idea is that everything is magnified. Greater internal demons to fight, greater external villains to fight, greater embodiment of virtues*, greater temptations of vices*. <br /> <br /> The idea with a superhero is that everything is just bigger. We want to see them overcome the worst that life has to offer. That's why. <br /> <br /> The monomyth, the Hero's Journey, is a story about overcoming challenges. That's the root of every hero story. Heroes are defined by the trials and tests that they face. Personal tragedies are more trials that they have to face. And, remember, humans love tragedies as much as they do comedies or success stories. <br /> <br /> <br /> * Also, recall that different eras and different countries don't share modern day values. So, the virtues a hero would need to embody change with time and place, as would the temptations they would need to overcome. What we might find terrible now wouldn't necessarily be considered bad in the past.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T08:48:30-07:00","id":551602,"title":"","content":"So, the difference in the stories is just that one's bigger than the other?<br /> <br /> ....so it's just the same story?<br /> <br /> ANd I like tragedies, but I don't see why the tragedy has to begin a story all the time. Going to Captain America*, he has tragedy, and they shape him as a character, but his story doesn't start with him going through a tragedy as an impetus for him to be the hero.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Again, I feel like Beast goes at that trope as a critical examination and deconstruction of it. It <i>is</i> problematic when you think about it. That said, I still quite like Spiderman**.<br /> <br /> *THe movies, anyways, I've never read the comics.<br /> **Particularly Spiderman Noir, which I <i>have</i> read.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 06-12-2015, 11:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T08:55:30-07:00","id":551607,"title":"","content":"See, my problem is though, is not that Hero is a good guy. (although I do think that making Beast-line hero into one-dimentional, integrity-less-than-4, ok-to-kill maniacs, and at the same time, not even intimidating to strong beasts, in a long term, makes them really lousy antagonists and actually hurts the game.)<br /> <br /> My problem is that the book seems to hammer on the opinion that the Beasts are so inherently not as bad as the Heroes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1229-torete","name":"torete","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1229_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"125"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T08:57:34-07:00","id":551614,"title":"","content":"Well, I haven't talked about Beasts, namely because the need to brainbarf isn't strong on them.<br /> <br /> That said, I do think Beasts are the more problematic of the two elements. I love them to death, but they are the trouble child.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T10:33:37-07:00","id":551827,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>torete</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/550680-arcane-s-meditation-on-heroes-from-primordial?p=551607#post551607\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">See, my problem is though, is not that Hero is a good guy. (although I do think that making Beast-line hero into one-dimentional, integrity-less-than-4, ok-to-kill maniacs, and at the same time, not even intimidating to strong beasts, in a long term, makes them really lousy antagonists and actually hurts the game.)<br /> <br /> My problem is that the book seems to hammer on the opinion that the Beasts are so inherently not as bad as the Heroes.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think I see the problem. You've got it into your head that heroes aren't completely unfettered lunatics that would, say, hijack a bus and crash it into a hospital to kill a Beast MD. That's why they're scary. They're not as inherently powerful, so they need to cheat. And they have no concept of &quot;collateral damage&quot;. They will cheerfully firebomb an apartment building, or shoot up a crowded shopping center, or any other unspeakable things in pursuit of their quarry.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T11:05:22-07:00","id":551853,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/550680-arcane-s-meditation-on-heroes-from-primordial?p=551827#post551827\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think I see the problem. You've got it into your head that heroes aren't completely unfettered lunatics that would, say, hijack a bus and crash it into a hospital to kill a Beast MD. That's why they're scary. They're not as inherently powerful, so they need to cheat. And they have no concept of &quot;collateral damage&quot;. They will cheerfully firebomb an apartment building, or shoot up a crowded shopping center, or any other unspeakable things in pursuit of their quarry.</div> </div> </div> </div>Their idea of cleaning a horse shed is flooding it with a river.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T11:07:27-07:00","id":551854,"title":"","content":"It's worth noting that not necessarily all of them would go to those measures or act without consideration to their Life (even with how subdued it is), but they are by nature predisposed to. One of their many tragic failings that nonetheless does not make their actions right.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":188,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","user":"YeOfLittleFaith","date":". 06-12-2015, 02:39 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T11:13:11-07:00","id":551860,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/550680-arcane-s-meditation-on-heroes-from-primordial?p=551827#post551827\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think I see the problem. You've got it into your head that heroes aren't completely unfettered lunatics that would, say, hijack a bus and crash it into a hospital to kill a Beast MD. That's why they're scary. They're not as inherently powerful, so they need to cheat. And they have no concept of &quot;collateral damage&quot;. They will cheerfully firebomb an apartment building, or shoot up a crowded shopping center, or any other unspeakable things in pursuit of their quarry.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Can you show me where it suggests this in the book? They're single-minded, but the book suggests they're really mostly after the Beast, and not just suicide bombers and mass murderers.<br /> <br /> The SAS actually has a Beast killing at least one person with a bomb and trying to kill others.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T11:57:03-07:00","id":551910,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/550680-arcane-s-meditation-on-heroes-from-primordial?p=551602#post551602\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, the difference in the stories is just that one's bigger than the other?<br /> <br /> ....so it's just the same story?</div> </div> </div> </div> To an extent. You can't exactly have a normal person tackle the same things that Capt.America, Thor, etc. But, yes, its the same Hero's Journey structure, the mono-myth applies to both.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And I like tragedies, but I don't see why the tragedy has to begin a story all the time. Going to Captain America*, he has tragedy, and they shape him as a character, but his story doesn't start with him going through a tragedy as an impetus for him to be the hero. </div> </div> </div> Correct me if I'm wrong, but wasn't his &quot;pre-hero tragedy&quot; being the target of bullies himself? Mind you, its not on par with Wayne's parents being killed, but its still there.<br /> <br /> Now, let me be clear. I think that the same stories are getting old. They do contain a lot of problematic material - hells, a lot of the old stories use &quot;the dangers of female sexuality&quot; as a moral or challenge. I think using Women in the Fridge trope is disgusting as a character development. There's a lot involved that's overdone. <br /> <br /> That said, when we're writing continual stories, such as Capt. America, we need to have a lot of material to work with. There's a LOT of writing involved. If anything, I would say that the problem comes down to the fact that the Hero's Journey itself is the problem. There's this idea that the Hero is the Chosen One. Everything revolves around them. Its a tired old plot that's been done to death, and is heavily focused on Manly Man type stories. Its about solving problems with violence, seeing the world as challenges to overcome.<br /> <br /> I think that the Hero's Journey is why we see so many games (role playing like D&amp;D, video games) and comics that revolve so heavily around violence. Its the &quot;default&quot; that we need to start writing away from, I think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T12:01:24-07:00","id":551919,"title":"","content":"We're actually going to add some stuff to the book about why Heroes do what they do, and metaphysically what the relationship between them and Beasts actually <i>is</i>. That will go hand-in-hand with the additional material on the Primordial Dream... there's a sort of an ecosystem there that we were going to explore later, but that we've decided to fold into the core.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Cavaliers of Mars Creator</b><br /> <b>Retired CofD Lead</b><br /> <br /> Check out my <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Rose%20Bailey\" target=\"_blank\">guides to </a><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/261142/BITE-ME-How-to-Write-Vampire?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> and my <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/4895/Fantasy-Heartbreaker?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">indie games</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","name":"Rose Bailey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2361"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T12:14:13-07:00","id":551961,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rose Bailey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/550680-arcane-s-meditation-on-heroes-from-primordial?p=551919#post551919\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We're actually going to add some stuff to the book about why Heroes do what they do, and metaphysically what the relationship between them and Beasts actually <i>is</i>. That will go hand-in-hand with the additional material on the Primordial Dream... there's a sort of an ecosystem there that we were going to explore later, but that we've decided to fold into the core.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would be lying if I said this doesn't make me excited!<br /> <br /> And that's getting <i>added</i> to the book? So no need to cut on page count for the material already there?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T12:31:41-07:00","id":552002,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rose Bailey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/550680-arcane-s-meditation-on-heroes-from-primordial?p=551919#post551919\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">there's a sort of an ecosystem there that we were going to explore later, but that we've decided to fold into the core.</div> </div> </div> </div>That sounds good.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T12:32:59-07:00","id":552003,"title":"","content":"Added. Fortunately, we still have room in our max page count guidelines.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Cavaliers of Mars Creator</b><br /> <b>Retired CofD Lead</b><br /> <br /> Check out my <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Rose%20Bailey\" target=\"_blank\">guides to </a><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/261142/BITE-ME-How-to-Write-Vampire?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> and my <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/4895/Fantasy-Heartbreaker?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">indie games</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","name":"Rose Bailey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2361"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T12:39:17-07:00","id":552017,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rose Bailey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/550680-arcane-s-meditation-on-heroes-from-primordial?p=552003#post552003\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Added. Fortunately, we still have room in our max page count guidelines.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's great to hear.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/188-yeoflittlefaith","name":"YeOfLittleFaith","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar188_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1159"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T13:08:42-07:00","id":552055,"title":"","content":"To me, the quintessential Hero is Ahab. Moby Dick makes a good Beast Soul, but that is only part of a Beast. The parallels don't work on that end. Of course, a Hero doesn't see past the Soul so Ahab as a Hero makes sense. The text says some Heroes will take hostages. They are also willing to sacrifice allies. I don't think they are really supposed to be committing mass murder. Players are supposed to love and hate Heroes. I think it would be in character for a Hero to talk a follower into going suicide bomber if it helps them kill a Beast. A Hero is actually an anti-hero. This contrasts to a Beast which is an anti-villain.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T13:13:53-07:00","id":552061,"title":"","content":"On further reading it does seem the extreme is mostly the domain of ravager heroes.<br /> <br /> That said, I think if you convinced a hero that the only way to kill a beast was to crash a plane into it, they would.<br /> <br /> And I now have a crazy idea for an anathema.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T13:19:04-07:00","id":552069,"title":"","content":"Weapondbound-Boeing 747","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T14:04:25-07:00","id":552154,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Diggs</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/550680-arcane-s-meditation-on-heroes-from-primordial?p=552055#post552055\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To me, the quintessential Hero is Ahab. Moby Dick makes a good Beast Soul, but that is only part of a Beast. The parallels don't work on that end. Of course, a Hero doesn't see past the Soul so Ahab as a Hero makes sense.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would like to have gotten a few anti-transcendentalist references into the book. Maybe in the expansion or a supplement. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Cavaliers of Mars Creator</b><br /> <b>Retired CofD Lead</b><br /> <br /> Check out my <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Rose%20Bailey\" target=\"_blank\">guides to </a><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/261142/BITE-ME-How-to-Write-Vampire?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> and my <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/4895/Fantasy-Heartbreaker?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">indie games</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","name":"Rose Bailey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2361"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T15:24:30-07:00","id":552270,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Rose Bailey</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/550680-arcane-s-meditation-on-heroes-from-primordial?p=552154#post552154\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I would like to have gotten a few anti-transcendentalist references into the book. Maybe in the expansion or a supplement. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Beast could certainly use a supplement or two. It has a great deal of depth, makes it difficult to grasp all the concepts as they were intended. Unfortunately, since you more or less released it, you can't now pull out huge chunks to save for a later book in the line to make space for all that you might like to create one book that has everything you need to play Beast (as intended). On the plus side I think the case has been made for why an early release can be a good thing. I can't imagine the hell you all would go through if you didn't get the reaction until after you sent it to backers. It sucks, but sometimes the game you intend just doesn't come across on the page the first time. At least, you get time you would not have otherwise gotten to shore it up. At worst, the Kickstarter doesn't go as well as desired, but the company's reputation will be intact. I'm sure Beast will work out as intended in the end. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T16:03:55-07:00","id":552303,"title":"","content":"Oh, we'll definitely have supplements. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> And the reaction hasn't been so bad. I'm willing to accept a certain... sharp analysis... as the price of writing things that are relevant. That may mean that we have to course-correct for intended meaning or to fix problems. But if people stop thinking seriously about what World of Darkness games mean, then I don't have a lot of reason left to write them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Cavaliers of Mars Creator</b><br /> <b>Retired CofD Lead</b><br /> <br /> Check out my <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Rose%20Bailey\" target=\"_blank\">guides to </a><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/261142/BITE-ME-How-to-Write-Vampire?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire</a> and my <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/browse/pub/4895/Fantasy-Heartbreaker?affiliate_id=453494&amp;src=OPPf\" target=\"_blank\">indie games</a>!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2-rose-bailey","name":"Rose Bailey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2361"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-12T23:23:04-07:00","id":552587,"title":"","content":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> Supplements are always nice. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> All the criticism I saw, including mine, was essentially reader interpretation not matching writer intent. I don't know how many other people recognized that was the real problem they had with Beast. To this day I'm not sure anyone has actually figured out what a Hero actually is. Every time someone tries to explain one I think the book has something to contradict that. I've heard people try to completely justify Heroes and others try to make them out to be irredeemable monsters. Some people think they are supposed to be the good guys while others think they are the bad guys. I'm thinking they are both, and neither. I mean that in a way less contradictory (and logic defying) than it sounds. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> I can wait with an open mind until they are clarified so that I can truly understand them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-06-13T15:26:54-07:00","id":553472,"title":"","content":"My interpretation of writer intent was that the Hero vs Beast conflict isn't so much Good vs. Evil or even Evil versus good, but rather Black and White vs Gray. The Hero sees the world only in absolutes, and the Beast sees only in relativistic terms, and both are colored by self-interest. Maybe the reason it appears inconsistent is because this could result in a wide array of different situations. Sometimes the Beast really is an awful monster that has to be put down. Sometimes the Hero is a sociopath. You get all these different combinations of sympathetic vs. unsympathetic, sympathetic vs. sympathetic, unsympathetic vs. unsympathetic, and everything in between. No neat little boxes, right?<br /> <br /> I was recently thinking that a good contemporary example of a Beast and his Hero was&quot; Breaking Bad.&quot; Walter White fears the powerlessness he feels in the face of his impending death and being unable to provide security for his family, so he gradually embraces the darker part of himself (Heisenberg) to, presumably, take care of his family. We gradually realize, however, that what he is really doing is satiating his Hunger for power. He never wants to feel powerless again. Enter the Hero, Hank. At first he comes off as a kind of a jerk and the audience is firmly on Walter/Heisenberg's side, but that gradually shifts and Hank becomes a sympathetic protagonist while Heisenburg becomes a genuine monster. <br /> <br /> Is the Hero the good guy and the Beast the villain, or is the Beast the good buy and the Hero the villain? I think the most accurate answer, to the consternation of many, is &quot;Yes.&quot;<br /> <br /> In any case, this is probably the most excited I've been for any RPG game line, and I've been a WoD fan for at least a decade.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6439-the-jellyfish","name":"The Jellyfish","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6439_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}